  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 4.1. Increased capacity of member States to create an enabling environment for entrepreneurship and sustainable enterprises</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">The in-depth analyses on enabling environment for sustainable enterprises resulted in policy papers and roadmaps for overcoming the challenges and improving the current state of business environment, namely, Entrepreneurship Ecosystem in North Macedonia - Market Assessment; Policy Proposal for Entrepreneurial Culture - Start-Up and Enterprise Support Services; Barriers for Access to Finance for MSMEs in North Macedonia - Stock of Existing Data and Knowledge. The policy papers and roadmaps were endorsed by the Economic and Social Council in 2021.
The survey carried out by the Organization of Employers of Macedonia and Business Confederation of Macedonia defined a set of actionable recommendations. The evidence collected through the survey was crucial in informing the Government decision to extend the wage subsidy schemes for enterprises, which was included in the new support package, adopted in February 2021. With the support of the European Union, the ILO provided support to tripartite constituents on the identification and formulation of recommendations to improve the business environment. This has been done in an incremental approach with the report published in 2019, providing an update on the key constraints identified during the first assessment of Enabling Environment or Sustainable Enterprises (EESE), involving the tripartite constituents and discussed within the Economic and Social Council (ESC). The analysis focused on priorities identified by the ESC (access to finance, entrepreneurship ecosystem (looking at skills)). 

In the last quarter of 2020, the ILO supported the Organization of Employers of Macedonia (OEM) and Business Confederation of Macedonia (BCM) in conducting a second countrywide enterprise survey with the aim to track business challenges and needs resulting from the protracted COVID-19 crisis. The survey helped to assess the effectiveness of economic emergency measures implemented through the four support packages launched by the Government. Additionally, the survey sought to identify the type of support expected under the fifth support package.</narrative>
   </description>
